ne out of three managers but  on the other hand i'm also not surprised  because i think uh you know particularly  for managers white particularly manager  is male they may not be sure  how to reach out they may find it risky  they're not sure what language to use  so what advice do you have for how  managers can make that call have that  conversation that they  themselves may find a little difficult  oh it's such a great question and it's  really important because if you  step up to be an ally it actually  matters a lot  we know that black women who had an ally  over the course of the pandemic are two  and a half times more likely  to see a fair workplace and to want to  stay and feel encouraged and motivated  so being an ally stepping up and  speaking out  and asking the basic question of what is  going on in your life and let me be  there let me understand and let me make  sure you still have opportunities in the  workplace  yeah rachel i want to jump back to you  you know one of the amazing things  that's happening right now  is that it's not just women who are in  their first position that are leaving  but women in senior roles who are  mid-career or even later  they're stepping back and that's a loss  because the kind of chain  of success is broken those women are  likely to be mentoring other women  how do you keep those women in those  jobs what do companies need to do to say  hey hey stick around i know it's hard  now but we need you  yeah bernard is the crux of this for a  lot of women so what we know  are senior leaders are facing a lot  right now they're running businesses  that are sometimes failing  employees are struggling and senior  level women are facing the same  stressors as senior level men  but then some they feel like they need  to work harder  they're more likely to be burned out and  they're more likely to think about  leaving the workforce and as you said we  cannot afford to lose senior level women  when there's more women in leadership  companies perform better they have  better cultures  and back to that mentorship and allyship  that lorena was just talking about  we know that senior level women are more  likely to show up for women of color  as allies mentors and sponsors so we  cannot afford to lose senior level women  organizations need to be doubling down  to make sure they feel supported  if leaders don't feel supported it's  hard for them to bring their best selves  and the empathy they need to make sure  other employees are getting the support  they need right now  lorena we're running out of time but  there's an important question i want to  get to uh to you in particular  california passed a law recently  requiring boards to have at least one  woman  and now even more recently the governor  of california  signed a law requiring those boards to  have a person of color i think it was an  executive director position  do you think quotas like that which  don't make it just a duty but a legal  responsibility are a good idea a good  way to reach these goals  well we've had quotas in europe before  and i think it's not so much the issue  of a quota per se  but do companies have clear targets and  goals  are you actually being specific about  what success looks like it's not just  more women  how many women and at what time period  and if you're not clear on your goal  how do you expect the rest of the  organization to fall suit  we know what gets measured gets watched  and what gets measured  gets done and as rachel said we also  know that diversity at the top  level of a management team and a board  creates better business performance  so why would you say no to that yeah  it's a good question companies sent  goals for everything else they put  numbers on it so why not put a number on  this  rachel thomas and lorena yee thank you  very much for being here guys  very important report
 

